@using UBookIt.Web.Rendering
@model IBookingFormView
@{
    var notice = Model.PrivacyNotice;
    var days = notice.RetentionDays;
}
@*
    What happens to the details this form is asking for. Rendered from ONE place because
    both flows collect contact details through _YourDetails, and a second copy is how the
    retention sentence would come to say two different things.

    THE FOUR STATEMENTS ARE FACTS ABOUT THIS PACKAGE, and every one of them is checkable
    against code in this repository: what is collected is what the fields above collect,
    why is what placement does with it, how long is `SiteBookingSettings.RetentionDays` —
    the same value the retention sweep acts on — and who can see it is the Sensitive data
    group check the backoffice applies. Nothing here asserts a lawful basis, a controller
    identity or a jurisdiction; those need a site's own judgement and live behind its own
    policy link.

    NO HEADING, deliberately. The document's heading outline is the site's, not the
    package's, and a heading inside a fieldset that already has a legend would be adding
    to an outline we do not own to solve a problem we do not have.

    NO CONTROL, deliberately. There is nothing to tick and nothing to agree to: the basis
    for holding a booker's details is performance of the booking, and an unrefusable
    tickbox would misrepresent that as consent — which would also imply a right to
    withdraw it and make the site's own records revocable.
*@
<div class="ubookit-privacy">
    @*
        WHY, AND THE SENTENCE CHANGES WITH THE SITE — because neither wording is true everywhere.

        This read "to hold your booking, to identify it, and to confirm it with you" until QA
        pointed at what the package actually did: it sent nothing at all, so on a default install
        the address confirmed nothing with anybody. It was corrected to "can contact you", which is
        true whether or not anything is ever sent.

        Now that the package CAN send, "can contact you" is the weaker half of a pair rather than
        the whole answer. A site that has turned booker messages on and can actually send them will
        send messages to this address, and a notice that declined to say so would understate
        the processing being performed on the very page collecting the address — the same fault as
        overstating it, in the other direction.

        SendsBookerEmail is the WHOLE condition, not the setting: it is
        BookingNotificationSettings.WillEmailBooker, the same expression the sending path is gated
        on. That is deliberate and load-bearing. A site that asks for messages on a host with no
        mail configuration sends nothing, and this must promise nothing there — writing the
        conjunction out again here is exactly how the sentence and the behaviour would drift apart.

        IT PROMISES MESSAGES, NOT A CONFIRMATION, and that generality is the fix for a defect
        rather than vagueness. It said "we will send your booking confirmation" until approval
        shipped; on a site with AutoConfirm off, placement sends "we have received your booking"
        and a booking that is then DECLINED never receives a confirmation at all — so the
        specific noun was a promise the package could not keep, on the very page collecting the
        address.

        The rejected alternative was to branch on AutoConfirm as well. That would put TWO
        conditions on a sentence whose design note above is that one condition governs both the
        saying and the sending — a second is a second way for them to drift, and it would still
        be wrong for the declined case. "Messages about your booking" is true under every
        combination, so the sentence cannot become false again without the sending itself
        changing.
    *@
    <p>
        @if (notice.SendsBookerEmail)
        {
            <text>
                We use your name and email address — and your phone number, if you give one — to hold
                and identify your booking. We will send messages about your booking to that email
                address, and use it to contact you about your booking.
            </text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>
                We use your name and email address — and your phone number, if you give one — to hold
                and identify your booking, and so that this site can contact you about it.
            </text>
        }
    </p>
    <p>
        @if (notice.HasRetentionPeriod)
        {
            @* Stated from the configured period, never authored. A sentence written by hand
               here could claim a period the code does not keep, which is the failure this
               whole notice exists to prevent. *@
            <text>
                We keep them for @days @(days == 1 ? "day" : "days") after your booking. After that
                we remove them permanently — the booking itself is kept, without your details.
            </text>
        }
        else
        {
            @* The DEFAULT rendering, because retention is off unless a site turns it on. Saying
               nothing here would omit one of the four statements on most installs, invisibly.
               So it says what is true of this configuration and promises nothing. *@
            <text>
                We keep them until we remove them. This site has not set a period for removing
                them automatically.
            </text>
        }
        In this site's backoffice they can be seen only by staff who have been given access to
        personal data.
    </p>
    @if (notice.HasPolicyUrl)
    {
        <p>
            <a href="@notice.PolicyUrl">Read this site's privacy policy</a>
        </p>
    }
</div>
